
✅ 1. Dual-Stack IP Support (IPv4 + IPv6)
What You Did:
Introduced detection and storage of both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses:

self.local_ip = self.get_local_ip()
self.local_ip_v4 = self.local_ip.get('ipv4')
self.local_ip_v6 = self.local_ip.get('ipv6')
Benefit:
Enables proper detection of ICMPv6 Echo Requests.

Future-proofs the tool for IPv6-only or mixed network deployments.

✅ 2. Centralized Time-Based Cleanup Function
What You Did:
Moved logic for removing expired entries into cleanup_old_entries().

Why It Matters:
Prevents repeated inline filtering in every packet handler.

Consolidates logic for easier tuning and consistency.

✅ 3. Background Cleanup Thread with Logging
What You Did:
Launched _run_cleanup_loop() in a daemon thread.

Logged how many stale entries are purged each cycle.

threading.Thread(target=self._run_cleanup_loop, daemon=True).start()
Benefit:
Keeps memory usage stable over time.

Provides insight into scan frequency and load.

✅ 4. Alert Deduplication with self._should_log()
What You Did:
Added suppression logic to avoid flooding logs with repeated alerts within 10 seconds.

Why It’s Important:
Prevents log flooding under repeated scan attacks.

Keeps user interface responsive and clean.

✅ 5. Packet Counters and Alert Label
What You Did:
Tracks total packet count.

Dynamically updates GUI alert label.

self.alert_label.config(text=f"Alerts: {alert_count} | Packets: {packet_count}")
Impact:
Gives real-time operational feedback to the user.

Useful for monitoring overall scan activity and verifying runtime health.

✅ 6. Queue-Driven Thread-Safe Logging System
What You Did:
Built a QueueHandler that feeds logs into a GUI-safe queue.

Enables live log display without thread collisions.

Why It’s Smart:
Prevents UI freezing due to log writes.

Ensures messages are processed cleanly in the main thread.

✅ 7. ARP and ICMP Toggle Controls
What You Did:
Added GUI toggles (via ToggleSwitch) to enable/disable detection at runtime.

Connected those toggles to internal state handlers.

self.arp_enabled = tk.BooleanVar(value=True)
self.icmp_enabled = tk.BooleanVar(value=True)
Benefit:
Empowers users to tailor scan detection live without restarting the tool.

✅ 8. IPv6-Aware ICMP Detection
What You Did:
Added ICMPv6 support with:

if pkt.haslayer(IPv6) and self.icmp_enabled:
    self._check_icmpv6(pkt)
Impact:
Essential for detecting IPv6 ping sweeps, which would otherwise go unnoticed.

✅ 9. Interface Selection
What You Did:
Allows users to monitor a specific interface or all.

iface = None if self.iface_var.get() == "[All Interfaces]" else self.iface_var.get()
Result:
Users gain granular control for segmented networks or VPN monitoring.

✅ 10. WHOIS Lookup with Thread Isolation
What You Did:
Performed WHOIS in a thread, updated GUI via after().

Value:
Avoids UI freeze during slow lookups or DNS failures.

✅ 11. Open Port Scanner with ThreadPool Control
What You Did:
Added safe multi-threaded TCP port scan logic with fallback.

max_workers = min(100, max(10, total_ports // 10))
Result:
Avoids overwhelming slower systems.

Balances performance with system stability.

✅ 12. GUI Tray Integration with Live Color Updates
What You Did:
System tray support with status-aware color indicators.

self.tray_icon.icon = self.create_image("yellow")
Why It’s Useful:
Allows minimized operation with persistent threat visibility.

✅ 13. Export and Clipboard Features
What You Did:
Allows log export to timestamped file and clipboard.

Usefulness:
Facilitates incident reporting or historical analysis.

✅ 14. Persistent, Rotating Log Files
What You Did:
Used RotatingFileHandler with size and date stamping.

file_handler = RotatingFileHandler(log_file_path, maxBytes=1024000, backupCount=3)
Security + Practicality:
Maintains long-term logs safely without consuming disk.

